    "comment": {
      "comments": [
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12330151/comment/12369908",
          "id": "12369908",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=yseeley%40gmail.com",
            "name": "yseeley@gmail.com",
            "key": "yseeley@gmail.com",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Yonik Seeley",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "body": "I'm not sure... I can think of examples either way.\nIf a field has a lot of synonyms, it will match more terms.\nShouldn't it's length be considered longer for the purposes of determining how specific it is to a query?\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=yseeley%40gmail.com",
            "name": "yseeley@gmail.com",
            "key": "yseeley@gmail.com",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Yonik Seeley",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "created": "2006-03-11T03:00:56.000+0000",
          "updated": "2006-03-11T03:00:56.000+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12330151/comment/12369918",
          "id": "12369918",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=randy%40zillow.com",
            "name": "randy@zillow.com",
            "key": "randy@zillow.com",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Randy Puttick",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Etc/UTC"
          },
          "body": "What you say would make sense if you thought that queries would contain several synonyms.  All my experience says that they don't; a person tends to use one synonym and it's the task of the search engine to make sure this matches synonymous terms in the documents (of course I come from a world where default boolean query is conjunctive so you'd never get any hits if you added a pile of synonyms into the query).",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=randy%40zillow.com",
            "name": "randy@zillow.com",
            "key": "randy@zillow.com",
            "avatarUrls": {
